{"name":"Magnificence","hex":"#7f556f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"magnificence","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Grandeur, brilliance, lavishness or splendor","The act of doing what is magnificent; the state or quality of being magnificent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magnificence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Magnificence (history of ideas)","blocks":[{"text":"The word magnificence comes from the Latin “magnum facere”, which means to do something great. The Latin word draws on the Greek “megaloprépeia”. This noun conveys the meaning of doing something great which is fitting or seemly to the circumstance. Magnificence is a philosophical, aesthetic, and socio-economic notion deeply rooted in Western culture since classical antiquity."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnificence_(history_of_ideas)"},{"title":"Catherine de' Medici's court festivals","blocks":[{"text":"A series of lavish and spectacular court entertainments, sometimes called magnificences, were laid on by Catherine de' Medici, the queen consort of France from 1547 to 1559 and queen mother from 1559 until her death in 1589. As wife of Henry II of France, Catherine showed interest in the arts and theatre, but it was not until she attained real political and financial power as queen mother that she began the series of tournaments and entertainments that dazzled her contemporaries and continue to fascinate scholars. Biographer Leonie Frieda suggests that \"Catherine, more than anyone, inaugurated the fantastic entertainments for which later French monarchs also became renowned\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Un_bal_%C3%A0_la_cour_d%27Henri_III.jpg/330px-Un_bal_%C3%A0_la_cour_d%27Henri_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_de'_Medici's_court_festivals"},{"title":"Magnificence (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Magnificence is a 1973 play by English playwright Howard Brenton. It premiered at the Royal Court Theatre and was next performed on the London stage in 2016, at the Finborough Theatre."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnificence_(play)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Fountain_chariot_from_the_Ballet_Comique_de_la_Reine%2C_1581.jpg/500px-Fountain_chariot_from_the_Ballet_Comique_de_la_Reine%2C_1581.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Fountain_chariot_from_the_Ballet_Comique_de_la_Reine%2C_1581.jpg/500px-Fountain_chariot_from_the_Ballet_Comique_de_la_Reine%2C_1581.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the Ballet Comique de la Reine, 1581, a fountain chariot carried Queen Louise and her ladies and musicians. Engraving by Jacques Patin.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFountain_chariot_from_the_Ballet_Comique_de_la_Reine%2C_1581.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Fontainebleau%2C_from_the_Valois_Tapestries.jpg/500px-Fontainebleau%2C_from_the_Valois_Tapestries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Fontainebleau%2C_from_the_Valois_Tapestries.jpg/500px-Fontainebleau%2C_from_the_Valois_Tapestries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" One of the Valois Tapestries, depicting entertainments at Fontainebleau in 1564, including the mock rescue of captive damsels from an enchanted island.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFontainebleau%2C_from_the_Valois_Tapestries.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Antoine_Caron%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne%2C_drawing.jpg/500px-Antoine_Caron%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne%2C_drawing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Antoine_Caron%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne%2C_drawing.jpg/500px-Antoine_Caron%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne%2C_drawing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Water Festival at Bayonne, a tapestry design by Antoine Caron, records entertainments laid on by Catherine de' Medici for the Franco-Spanish summit meeting of 1565.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntoine_Caron%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne%2C_drawing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg/500px-Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg/1280px-Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg/500px-Valois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Water Festival at Bayonne, the finished tapestry version of 1580–81","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AValois_tapestry%2C_Water_Festival_at_Bayonne.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9%2C_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg/500px-La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9%2C_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9%2C_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg/1280px-La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9%2C_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse